  "enhanced_information": "Choledocholithiasis, or the presence of gallstones in the common bile duct, leads to bile duct obstruction. This obstruction prevents the normal excretion of conjugated bilirubin into the intestine, causing it to accumulate in the bloodstream. The increased levels of conjugated bilirubin in the blood result in jaundice, characterized by yellowing of the skin and eyes. The most relevant pathophysiological mechanism explaining jaundice in choledocholithiasis is the disorder in the conduction of bilirubin to the duodenum, as the obstruction prevents bilirubin from reaching the intestine. This aligns with the consensus that jaundice in such cases is due to impaired excretion of bilirubin, not increased production or defects in uptake or conjugation by the liver. The retrieved evidence paths support this by indicating that choledocholithiasis causes cholestasis and obstruction, which are directly related to the impaired excretion of bilirubin.",
  "analysis": "Choledocholithiasis refers to the presence of gallstones in the common bile duct. This condition can lead to obstruction of bile flow, which results in cholestasis. Cholestasis is a condition where bile cannot flow from the liver to the duodenum, leading to a buildup of bilirubin in the blood. This buildup causes jaundice, which is characterized by yellowing of the skin and eyes. The pathophysiological mechanism behind the jaundice in choledocholithiasis is therefore a disorder in the conduction of bilirubin to the duodenum, as the bile duct obstruction prevents bilirubin from being excreted into the intestine.",
